  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called strabismus) can feel confusing or awkward, but it doesn't have to be. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 1. **Look at the Eye That Is Looking at You**\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be, it will feel to them as though you are staring at their lazy eye.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 2. **Remember \u2014 They ARE Making Eye Contact With You**\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one of their eyes is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else, which can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact and don't want to pay attention. Keep in mind that both their eyes are, in fact, looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 3. **Don't Overthink It \u2014 Just Carry On**\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 4. **Try Slightly Blurring Your Focus**\nIf there's something unusual on someone's face that you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better. This can help you maintain a natural-looking gaze without fixating on either eye.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 5. **Don't Constantly Shift Your Gaze Nervously**\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about them start to form your dominant image. Relax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 6. **If It's Someone You Know Well, It's OK to Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate because of a friend or coworker's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something lighthearted like \"sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. However, don't use this approach with a salesperson, flight attendant, or someone you've just met.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 7. **For Strangers, Focus on Another Part of Their Face**\nWhen you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious about their eye. If you really feel uneasy, find another point of their face to focus on and wrap up the conversation as quickly as possible.\n\n---\n\nThe most important thing is to be respectful and natural. People with lazy eyes are very much aware of how others react, so staying calm and engaged goes a long way in making the interaction comfortable for both of you.",
